00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:05.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  are you ready for the uk transition  number one buying from or selling to the  eu  hello my name is jane rose and i'm head  of international trade at the essex  chambers of commerce  we've created four bite size videos  about the uk transition  or brexit these have been commissioned  by rochford district council  to help businesses prepare for the  changes to trading with the eu  we will be covering buying from or  selling goods to the eu  key dates and stages what to do to  prepare to continue trading with eu from  january  and finally where to find resources and  support to help you get ready  there are web links and references  listed in the video description  in this video i'll be talking about what  will happen if you buy goods from or  sell products to  the eu so let's get started  as you'll be aware we are no longer in  the eu and on the 31st of december  2020 the current transition period will  end  you've probably heard this mentioned so  many times but what does this actually  mean for your business  well up until the 31st of january you'll  be able to carry on buying and selling  goods  to and from the eu in the same way that  you do now  but from the 1st of january that will  all change  the transition period will end and there  will be a uk customs border between the  uk  and the eu to put it simply when you're  buying and selling goods to and from the  eu it will be classed as exporting  and importing this is the same as  trading with say  the middle east or india you may have  heard in the news about a free trade  agreement between the uk  and eu regardless of whether there is  agreement between the two parties  the way you trade with the eu will  change from the 1st of january  so if you haven't already done so you  need to prepare now  here are some action points to get you  started on your preparations  meet with key people in your business  make sure that they understand  there will be changes that will affect  the business  it's a good idea to start by assessing  what you buy from and sell to the eu  then get some in-house knowledge or  training on customs processes  review your existing processes and  future requirements  it's important to learn about customs  declarations  as this is the single biggest  requirement and change impacting how you  will trade with eu  and how the eu will trade with the uk  you'll need to find out how to classify  your goods using the new uk global  tariff and exporters trade tool  it's important to speak with your  logistics and transport providers  as there are changes and new  requirements for them too  lastly speak with your eu customers and  suppliers  are they prepared it's important to know  as they'll have to make similar  preparations for trade to go ahead  smoothly  we know there are a lot of essex  businesses that are well versed with  exporting worldwide  but it's very different to trading with  the eu so i can't stress enough that you  need to plan ahead  it will take time to understand what you  need to do but once you've got the hang  of it  you'll be able to import and export  globally and make the most of your  trading opportunities  however if you do nothing your goods  will not be allowed to pass through the  border  and your business will be disrupted so i  do urge you to act now  the government have introduced three  stages to the process to help you  prepare  they're based around three key dates the  first of january the first of april  and the first of july 2021. in the next  video we'll take a closer look at what  these key dates mean for you  please do contact us at the essex  chambers if you need our help  and explore the web links in the video  description for further information  thank you so much for listening  [Music]  you

